Thanks for looping me in. A few initial thoughts, with the caveat that I haven't been close to this project so this is a read from the outside rather than a firm recommendation yet.

The note leans toward starting from scratch since the framework never got past alpha. Before we default to a rewrite, I'd want to pressure-test that a little. "Stalled at alpha" usually means one of two very different things: it got deprioritized and the bones are fine, or the architecture was fighting the team (flaky waits, brittle locators, weak device handling) and a rewrite is the honest call. Those look the same from the outside but point to opposite decisions.

Two things actually work in our favor here. It's built on Appium, which is cross-platform by design, so it's not locked to one OS the way an Espresso or XCUITest build would be — meaning reusability for your area comes down to code quality rather than platform fit. And the emulator + real-device infrastructure and the GitHub Actions pipeline are already in place. Wiring CI to real devices is the genuinely time-consuming part of mobile automation, so if that plumbing works, that's real preserved value regardless of what happens to the test code on top.

My suggestion: rather than a blind keep-or-rebuild call, let me spend an hour or two looking at the repo — the CI workflow, the driver/capabilities layer, and a sample screen object. That's enough to tell us what's actually reusable and give you a keep/toss read with an effort estimate, instead of a coin flip.

Happy to get started whenever you can point me to the repo or the report.
